=== Handgrip Dynamometry

Handgrip strength testing is the cheapest functional biomarker in this chapter: a digital dynamometer, ten maximal contractions, a 60-minute break, and a second identical session. It requires no laboratory, no exercise laboratory, and no recovery bed. The construct it measures is deliberately narrow — maximal voluntary force, the decline of that force within a session (fatigue ratio), and the recovery of force after an hour (recovery ratio).

The evidence supports a split verdict, and both halves matter:

    - *As a severity and trajectory measure*: repeated HGS separates ME/CFS from healthy controls with a large effect on absolute force and moderate effects on fatigue and recovery ratios @Jakel2021HGS @Mihatsch2026HandgripCYP @Wallraven2026HandgripPCS.
    - *As a diagnostic test*: it does not reliably separate ME/CFS from other fatiguing conditions, and no threshold has been externally validated @Mihatsch2026HandgripCYP @Wallraven2026HandgripPCS @DazaArana2026ML.

A third question — whether the deficit is peripheral (muscle) or central (drive) — is unresolved and changes what the test means @oq:handgrip-peripheral-vs-central. A speculative vascular reading of the recovery signal is carried separately at @spec:handgrip-microvascular.

