#synthesis(title: [Handgrip Strength: A Monitoring Tool, Not a Diagnostic Test])[
Three independent cohorts converge on the same narrow conclusion. First, a performance deficit is real and reproducible: adults and children with ME/CFS generate less grip force and recover more slowly after an hour than controls @Jakel2021HGS @Mihatsch2026HandgripCYP @Wallraven2026HandgripPCS. Second, the deficit correlates only modestly with symptom severity (r ≈ −0.3 in the post-COVID cohort, and no correlation with PEM duration in the pediatric cohort) and does not separate ME/CFS from other fatiguing illness at the individual level @Mihatsch2026HandgripCYP @Wallraven2026HandgripPCS @DazaArana2026ML. Third, the mechanism behind the deficit is undetermined: peripheral muscle limitation and reduced central drive both remain compatible with the published data @oq:handgrip-peripheral-vs-central, and the deconditioning explanation has not been excluded by any study that adjusted for muscle mass and activity @oq:handgrip-deconditioning-vs-disease.

What follows for use is a strict separation of purposes. As a monitoring and phenotyping instrument, repeated handgrip testing is cheap, feasible, and quantified, and it can document functional impairment for clinical or administrative purposes @cf:handgrip-functional-monitor. As a diagnostic instrument it fails, and a normal result carries no exclusionary weight @lim:handgrip-diagnostic-discrimination. The most important open question is temporal rather than mechanistic: whether a fall in recovery ratio precedes a PEM episode within a patient @oq:handgrip-prospective-pem-tracking. Until that is answered, the test describes capacity at the moment of measurement and nothing more.

*Consequence:* Handgrip testing gives clinicians and patients a cheap objective number for a condition that otherwise lacks one, but it must be used to track a patient over time — not to decide whether the patient is ill, and not to judge whether the patient is trying.
] <syn:handgrip-fatigability-model>
